  "fingerprintThisVault": "Fingerprint opens this vault",
  "secIntro": "Everything in the vault stays on this phone and is encrypted before it is written. No server, no account: nobody else can open your vault, not even us.",
  "sec1Title": "Encrypted on the phone",
  "sec1Body": "Every photo, note and file has its own key and is encrypted before it is written. Big files are encrypted in small chunks; change or cut a single chunk and the app notices and refuses to open it.",
  "sec2Title": "Two things open a vault",
  "sec2Body": "You choose the backup password. The Secret Key is a random string the app makes and keeps in the phone's secure storage. Someone with a copy of the vault but not the Secret Key cannot even start guessing the password, and every guess costs a lot of memory and over a second.",
  "sec3Title": "Quick to open on this phone",
  "sec3Body": "Your PIN and Face ID only unlock a copy of the vault key kept for this phone. Copy the vault to another device and the PIN is useless. From the fifth wrong PIN you wait 1, 5, 15, then 60 minutes; the app never wipes your data.",
  "sec4Title": "Nothing goes to the internet",
  "sec4Body": "This version never connects to the network: no account, no ads, no analytics, no crash reports. On Android and Mac, the system gives the app no network permission. On iPhone and other computers, the app blocks every connection itself, and every release is checked automatically. No server holds a copy or a password, so nobody can recover it for you either: forget the backup password, or lose both the phone and the Emergency Kit, and the data is gone.",
  "sec5Title": "While the vault is open",
  "sec5Body": "The vault locks as soon as you leave the app and after a few minutes without a touch. Lists show note titles only, secrets are masked, copied text is cleared after 60 seconds. Android blocks screenshots and recordings; iPhone covers the vault while the screen is recorded.",
  "sec6Title": "Backups",
  "sec6Body": "A backup is one encrypted file you can keep anywhere. Opening it on a new phone takes both the backup password and the Secret Key from the Emergency Kit: keep the kit somewhere safe, away from your phone.",
  "secTech": "Technical details",
  "secTechBody": "Encryption: XChaCha20-Poly1305 in 64 KB chunks, a random key per item.\nKey from the password: Argon2id (phones 128 MB, computers 512 MB) combined with a 128-bit Secret Key through HKDF-SHA256.\nPIN and biometrics: a copy of the vault key wrapped by a key in the Keychain / Android Keystore.\nCore in Rust with unsafe code forbidden; open source (GPL-3.0) at github.com/cozy-pi/cozysafe.\nNo independent security audit yet.",
